course: dictionary
lesson: 12351
title: Credentials: proof and trust
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Credentials is a word about proof. It shows that someone is trustworthy, qualified or allowed to do something. Read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «The guard checked his credentials at the gate.» The clues are checked and at the gate. He showed something that proves who he is, such as an ID badge, so credentials means papers or badges that prove identity.
explain: «Never share your login credentials with anyone.» Login tells you this is about a username and password. Swap in your meaning: «Never share your username and password with anyone» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «Her credentials as a doctor are excellent.» Here credentials means her training and experience, which show she is suitable for the job. Always check which meaning fits the sentence.
fact: Credentials is usually a plural noun, so it is written with an s.
fact: «Credentials» can mean qualifications, training and experience that show someone is suitable or trustworthy.
fact: «Credentials» can also mean documents that prove who you are, like an ID badge or press pass.
fact: In computing, «credentials» are your username and password, used to log in.
fact: Never share your login credentials, and real companies never ask for them by email or message; that trick is called phishing.
exercise: «The guard checked his credentials at the gate.» What are credentials here? | A loud alarm | *An ID badge that proves who he is | A cup of tea for the guard | A kind of metal gate
exercise: «Her credentials as a doctor are excellent.» What do her credentials show? | *Her training and experience make her suitable for the job | She lives close to the hospital | She has many friends at the hospital | She is a very fast runner
exercise: Which phrase means the username and password you use to log in? | Green credentials | Hobby credentials | Impressive credentials | *Login credentials
test: «Before you trust the advice, check the expert's credentials.» What should you check? | How loudly the expert speaks | The colour of the expert's shoes | *The expert's training and experience
test: An email asks you to send your login credentials to fix your account. What should you do? | Send them to a friend first, just to be safe | *Do not send them, because real companies never ask for them this way | Send them quickly so the account gets fixed
exam: Which sentence uses credentials to mean proof of identity? | *«The guard asked to see her credentials before she came in» | «Her credentials as a teacher are impressive» | «Never share your login credentials» | «The car has excellent green credentials»
